course: medicine
lesson: 40
title: Growing and sport
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A growing child is not a small grown-up. Near the ends of your long bones are growth plates, softer zones where new bone is made, and they keep working until about the late teens.
explain: When you shoot up 8 cm in a year, your bones race ahead and your muscles and your brain's sense of where your feet are need time to catch up, so you may trip over the ball for a while. It passes.
explain: Some sports aches belong to growing: a sore bump under the kneecap or sore heels can come from a tendon tugging on a growing spot. They settle when growing stops, and a GP can help meanwhile.
explain: Look at any Under-11 team photo: the tallest kid might be 25 cm taller than the smallest, just because growth spurts start at different ages. Messi was a small boy and became one of the greatest players ever, so judge players by skill, not size.
fact: When bones grow fast, muscles and the brain's sense of body position need time to catch up, so many children are clumsy for a while during a growth spurt.
fact: Children's long bones have growth plates near their ends; they are weaker than the bone around them, so pain near a joint after sport that doesn't settle should be seen by a doctor.
fact: Most growth plates close in the late teens, at about 17 or so, and adults don't have open growth plates.
fact: A sore bump just below the kneecap in sporty children of about 10 to 15 is often Osgood-Schlatter: a tendon pulling on a growing spot of the shin bone; it settles once growing finishes, though the bump may stay.
fact: Heel pain in sport in children of about 8 to 14 is often Sever's disease, the Achilles tendon pulling on the heel's growth plate; rest, cushioned shoes and less running on hard ground help.
fact: Growing pains are aches in both legs, usually in the evening or night and gone by morning; despite the name they aren't caused by bones growing.
fact: Pain in only one leg, a limp, swelling, fever, or pain during the day is not ordinary growing pains and needs a doctor.
fact: Children start growth spurts at different ages, so classmates of the same age can differ a lot in size, and late growers often catch up.
fact: Children born early in the school year can be almost a year older than classmates; some academies now group young players by maturity rather than only age, called bio-banding.
fact: As a boy, Lionel Messi was treated for a growth hormone problem and grew to about 1.70 m; Peter Crouch, at about 2.01 m, also played for England.
fact: Every body size can do sport: gymnast Simone Biles is about 1.42 m, and Muggsy Bogues played in the NBA at about 1.60 m.
fact: Bigger, heavier bodies are a real strength in some positions, like rugby forwards such as a prop; teasing someone about their size is never OK.
exercise: Ethan grew 8 cm this year and keeps tripping over the ball. Why? | His muscles and sense of balance are catching up with his bones | He has suddenly become bad at football and should give it up now | The pitch really is moving | His bones have got weaker as they stretched
exercise: Ethan was 135 cm. After growing 8 cm, how tall is he? | 143 cm | 127 cm | 138 cm | 150 cm
exercise: What is a growth plate? | A softer zone near a bone's end where it grows | A big plate of food for growing kids after sport | A metal plate put inside broken bones | A shield on the front of the knee
exercise: Why do doctors check children's injuries near joints carefully? | Growth plates there are weaker than the bone around them | Children's bones are made of something rather like soft rubber | Children's joints have no real bones in them yet | Joints in children never heal
exercise: At about what age do most growth plates close? | The late teens, about 17 | About age 5, before school | About age 40, in middle age | They never close, we grow all our lives
exercise: Connor in Ottawa has a sore bump below his kneecap after hockey. What is it often? | A tendon pulling on a growing spot of the shin | Arthritis that ends his sport for good, like an old man's | A broken kneecap from a fall | A bruise from his own skates
exercise: Anaya's heels hurt in football boots, worst on hard pitches. What is it often in kids of 8 to 14? | A tendon pulling on the heel's growth plate | Only boots that are too new and stiff, nothing else | A broken heel bone from kicking | Flat feet that never get any better
exercise: What helps sore heels in a growing footballer? | Rest, cushioned shoes and less running on hard ground | Running more on concrete to toughen the heels up | Playing barefoot on hard pitches every day | Wearing boots two sizes too small to hold the heel tightly in place
exercise: Rory's legs ache at night after sport but he's fine by morning. What are growing pains? | Aches in both legs at night, gone by morning | Pain exactly where the bones are growing longer | A sign that a bone has broken during sport | Pain that lasts all day and night
exercise: Which needs a doctor, not just "growing pains"? | Pain in one leg with a limp or swelling | Aches in both legs at bedtime | Tired, heavy legs after a big match | Legs that are fine again the next morning
exercise: In the team photo the tallest is 155 cm and the smallest 130 cm. Difference? | 25 cm | 285 cm | 35 cm | 15 cm
exercise: Why can children of the same age be so different in size? | Growth spurts start at different ages | Tall children eat more carrots and drink more milk | Short children don't do enough sport at all | Size is fixed at birth and never changes
exercise: Freddie, born in September, and Max, born in August, are in the same school year. About how much older is Freddie? | About 11 months | About 1 week | About 3 years, a whole school stage | They are exactly the same age
exercise: What is bio-banding? | Grouping young players by maturity, not only age | Banning tall players from youth teams | Giving small players a head start in races | Testing players' blood before games for vitamins
exercise: Lucas is the shortest on his team. What's a true example? | Messi, about 1.70 m, became one of the greatest players | No player under 1.80 m has ever played professional football | Messi was always the tallest in his team | Only tall players ever reach the World Cup
exercise: Tom, the biggest kid in Year 5, joins rugby. Where can a big body be a real strength? | As a forward, like a prop | Nowhere, big kids can't really do any sport well | Only as a referee or a linesman | Only in a sport with no running in it
test: Ethan: "Ref, the pitch is sus, it's moving!" Six months later, what will likely happen? | His co-ordination comes back as his body catches up | He'll be clumsy forever and never get better | He'll have to quit football for good | He'll shrink back to his old height
test: Sana: "Growth plate? Sounds sus." What would happen if doctors treated her wrist like an adult's? | They might miss an injury to a weaker growing zone | Nothing, kids' bones are just small adult bones | Her wrist would grow faster than the other one | Her bones would stop growing at once
test: Connor: "My knee's busted, eh? No more hockey ever." What's the better plan? | Tell a parent, see a GP, ease off when sore | Quit all sport for the rest of his life | Play through any pain until it stops | Wrap it up tight and run more to strengthen it
test: Teammate: "You just need new boots, innit." Anaya's heels still hurt in the new ones. What's likely? | It's often the heel's growth plate in growing kids | The new boots must be the cause too, so swap again | She must be faking it to get out of training | Heel pain means her feet stopped growing
test: Rory, at breakfast: "Grand now, Mam." But what if he had pain in one leg all day and a fever? | That needs a doctor | That's just growing pains, nothing to worry about | That's normal after a lot of sport | It means he's growing taller quickly
test: The tall lad sneers, "Shortie can't even reach the ball, LOL." What's the fact? | Late growers often catch up, and skill matters more | The biggest kid will always be the best | Short kids should quit sport now | Height at 10 decides your sport forever
test: Coach: "Freddie's just better, Max is rubbish." Max is 11 months younger. What's fairer? | Give younger players time, many catch up | Drop all the summer-born kids from the team | Only pick the tallest kids every time | Never pick younger players again for matches
test: Lucas: "I'm cooked, bro, I'll never go pro." His coach mentions two England and Argentina stars. Which pair shows that size isn't everything? | Messi about 1.70 m and Crouch about 2.01 m | Two 2-metre-tall strikers who both scored loads | Two players of exactly the same height | No famous short players exist
test: A lad says "Basketball's not for short-arses, mate." What's a true counterexample? | Muggsy Bogues played in the NBA at about 1.60 m | No NBA player was ever under 2 m | Short people can't jump high enough | Basketball has a height rule for pros
test: Tall Freya says she's too lanky for gymnastics. What's the best advice? | There's a sport for every body; try lots and pick what you love | Tall people are banned from gymnastics | She should only ever do basketball or netball | She should try to stop herself growing
test: Kids call Tom "the fridge" until he's their rugby prop. What's true about teasing about size? | It's never OK, and every body is good at something | It's fine if he's big and doesn't mind it | It makes people tougher and better at sport | It's OK as long as you're only joking
exam: Identify the anatomical feature, present in children but closed in adults, at which the long bones increase in length. | The growth plate | The cap of the knee joint | The Achilles tendon | The spinal cord in the back
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning so-called growing pains. | They affect both legs at night and resolve by morning | They arise precisely at the sites of bone growth | They invariably indicate a fracture of the bone | They persist throughout the day and night without relief
exam: It is given that one pupil measures one hundred and fifty-five centimetres and another one hundred and thirty centimetres. Determine the difference in stature. | Twenty-five centimetres | Thirty-five centimetres | Two hundred and eighty-five centimetres | Fifteen centimetres
exam: Ascertain the most accurate characterisation of temporary clumsiness during a rapid growth spurt. | A normal lag of muscle and balance behind bone growth | A permanent loss of sporting ability | Evidence of a disorder of the brain or nerves | A consequence of excessive sleep during the growth spurt
